      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given that $10, a, b, ab$ form an arithmetic progression, let's denote the first term as $10$, the second as $a$, the third as $b$, and the fourth as $ab$.\n\nBy definition of arithmetic progression, the difference between consecutive terms must be equal. Thus, we have the equations:\n$$a-b=\\text{common difference}$$\n$$b-ab=\\text{common difference}$$\n\nLet's solve these two equations simultaneously. Adding $b$ times the first equation to the second equation, we get:\n$$b(1+a)-b=ab-\\text{common difference}+10$$\n$$ba=ab-ab-ab+10$$\n$$ab=10+b$$\n\nNow, since $b-ab$ must also be equal to the common difference, we have:\n$$0=(b-ab)$$\n$$0=b(ab-10)$$\n\nThis reveals two possible cases for $b$: $b=0$ or $b=10$.\n\nFor the first case, $b=0$, substituting into our equation $ab=10$ gives us:\n$$0\\cdot a=10$$\nwhich is not possible since no real numbers satisfy this. Hence, this case contains no valid pairs.\n\nFor the second case, $b=10$, substituting into our equation $ab=10$ gives us:\n$$a\\cdot 10=10$$\n$$a=1$$\n\nThus, we have one valid solution $(1,10)$.\n\nSince we are looking for pairs $(a,b)$, the solution set is $\\boxed{(1,10)}$.",
