      "text": "To determine the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) of real numbers such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) is an arithmetic progression, we need to ensure that the difference between consecutive terms is constant. This means:\n\n\\[a - 10 = b - a = ab - b\\]\n\nWe will solve these equations step-by-step. First, let's rewrite the equations for clarity:\n\n1. \\(a - 10 = b\\)\n2. \\(b - a = ab - b\\)\n\nFrom equation (1), we can express \\(b\\) in terms of \\(a\\):\n\\[a = b + 10\\]\n\nNow substitute \\(b = a - 10\\) into equation (2):\n\\[(a - 10) - a = a(a - 10) - (a - 10)\\]\nSimplify this:\n\\[-10 = a^2 - 10a - a + 10\\]\n\\[-10 = a^2 - 11a + 10\\]\n\nRearrange the equation to form a standard quadratic equation:\n\\[a^2 - 11a + 20 = 0\\]\n\nNow, solve this quadratic equation for \\(a\\):\n\\[\na = \\frac{11 \\pm \\sqrt{121 - 80}}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\na = \\frac{11 \\pm \\sqrt{41}}{2}\n\\]\n\nSince the quadratic equation gives us two solutions for \\(a\\), they also provide us with corresponding values for \\(b\\) using \\(b = a - 10\\):\n\n1. For \\(a = \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{41}}{2}\\):\n\\[b = \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{41}}{2} - 10 = \\frac{11 + \\sqrt{41} - 20}{2} = \\frac{-9 + \\sqrt{41}}{2}\\]\n\n2. For \\(a = \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{41}}{2}\\):\n\\[b = \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{41}}{2} - 10 = \\frac{11 - \\sqrt{41} - 20}{2} = \\frac{-9 - \\sqrt{41}}{2}\\]\n\nThus, the pairs \\((a, b)\\) are:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{11 + \\sqrt{41}}{2}, \\frac{-9 + \\sqrt{41}}{2}\\right) \\text{ and } \\left(\\frac{11 - \\sqrt{41}}{2}, \\frac{-9 - \\sqrt{41}}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\nLet's verify these calculations with Python to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve, sqrt\r\n\r\n# Define the variables\r\na = symbols('a')\r\nb = symbols('b')\r\n\r\n# Given equations based on the arithmetic progression\r\neq1 = Eq(a - 10, b)\r\neq2 = Eq(b - a, a*b - b)\r\n\r\n# Solve eq1 for b\r\nb_expr = solve(eq1, b)[0]\r\n\r\n# Substitute b in eq2\r\neq2_substituted = eq2.subs(b, b_expr)\r\n\r\n# Simplify and solve for a\r\na_solutions = solve(eq2_substituted, a)\r\n\r\n# Calculate corresponding b values\r\npairs = [(a_val, a_val - 10) for a_val in a_solutions]\r\nprint((a_solutions, pairs))\n```\n```output\n([11/2 - sqrt(41)/2, sqrt(41)/2 + 11/2], [(-sqrt(41)/2 + 11/2, -sqrt(41)/2 + 11/2 - 10), (11/2 - sqrt(41)/2, -10 + sqrt(41)/2 + 11/2)])\n```\nThe solutions for \\(a\\) are:\n\n\\[\na = \\frac{11}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad a = \\frac{11}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\n\\]\n\nCorresponding to these values of \\(a\\), the pair \\((b)\\) values computed are:\n\n1. For \\(a = \\frac{11}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\):\n\\[\nb = \\frac{11}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2} - 10 = \\frac{11 - 20 - \\sqrt{41}}{2} = \\frac{-9 - \\sqrt{41}}{2}\n\\]\n\n2. For \\(a = \\frac{11}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\):\n\\[\nb = \\frac{11}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2} - 10 = \\frac{11 - 20 + \\sqrt{41}}{2} = \\frac{-9 + \\sqrt{41}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the pairs \\((a, b)\\) are indeed:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{11}{2} - \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}, \\frac{-9 - \\sqrt{41}}{2}\\right) \\text{ and } \\left(\\frac{11}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}, \\frac{-9 + \\sqrt{41}}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
